{"kind":"assert","text":"Follow-up to overhead measurement finding 3965e230: the checker investigated whether retained phase timings and configured caps could prove a conservative worker-lifetime ratio without a new broad measurement. They cannot establish that bound at the corrected consolidated head. Vitest 4.1.11 removes a completed task, starts asynchronous runner termination and can schedule new tasks before prior runners finish shutting down. The active-task maxWorkers limit is therefore not a proven cap on simultaneous live runner lifetimes, including between project groups; root checked cli-api.CnMVyzaz.js lines3486–3562 and3759–3798. Separate-project 641 timings also do not supply consolidated per-project intervals. A baseline lower bound from Vitest's nonoverlapping phases is plausible, but the complete990.8 subtotal also contains Git whole-suite coordinator time and a different UI measurement; it is not automatically one uniform lifetime bound. Cloudflare pool runners, runtime processes and application isolates need explicit units rather than interchangeable labels. The normal edit-cycle worker measurement remains separate. This explains why a one-worker suite command or cap-times-wall estimate should keep its actual boundary, not be called observed aggregate lifetimes. Retain completed attributable output and honest limits; no duplicate root gate, cold baseline or old sweep is introduced.","body":{"scope":"Read-only investigation of cheaper retained-data worker bounds; no extra validation mandate","source_head":"15680192f93b6268f405b52b21ce0874060bc193"}}